export const modernPerspective = [
  'Berry-phase polarization comparisons require an explicit branch choice. In practice the reliable object is the continuous change in polarization along a specified adiabatic path that remains insulating; if the relevant gap closes, the assumed insulating branch cannot simply be continued through that point.',
  'A Born effective charge is a response tensor defined by the change of macroscopic polarization under an atomic displacement. It is therefore distinct from a formal oxidation state, Bader charge, orbital population, or other static charge partition, even when one of those chemical pictures helps interpret the result.',
  'Inversion symmetry quantizes polarization modulo the polarization quantum. In one dimension the condition P ≡ −P modulo the quantum permits two classes, conventionally represented by 0 and half a quantum. The distinction is already geometric and becomes part of the topological language developed in the following chapters.',
];

export const synthesis =
  'Chapter 24 completes Part V by turning polarization from an ambiguous cell dipole into a bulk geometric quantity. The position operator makes an absolute dipole ill-defined in a periodic crystal, whereas the current carried during an adiabatic insulating evolution gives a well-defined change in polarization. Quantum mechanics rewrites that transported charge as a Berry phase of the occupied subspace, and Chapter 23 then supplies an equivalent real-space picture through the motion of Wannier centers. The same framework explains Born effective charges and spontaneous polarization, provides a quantitative measure of electronic localization, and reveals why a cyclic evolution can pump an integer charge. Because polarization is defined only modulo a lattice quantum, its branch structure already contains the geometric ingredients needed for Part VI, where Berry curvature, Chern numbers, bulk–boundary correspondence, and topological insulators become the central objects.';
---

<section aria-labelledby="section-24-1-overview">
  <h2 id="section-24-1-overview">24.1 Overview</h2>
  <p>
    Polarization is a bulk property of an insulator, but the familiar finite-system picture of “charge times position” becomes unreliable when the material is infinite and periodic. The difficulty is physical rather than merely technical: translating charge by a lattice vector returns the same bulk crystal, while a naive dipole formula appears to assign a different value.
  </p>
  <p>
    The distinction between metals and insulators clarifies the problem. A metal screens a static macroscopic electric field by transporting free charge over macroscopic distances. An insulator can sustain such a field and can carry a transient polarization current when its electronic state, atomic coordinates, or strain are varied. The measurable bulk quantity is therefore naturally connected to current and to changes in polarization.
  </p>
  <aside class="callout" aria-label="From response to polarization">
    <p><strong>Conceptual bridge</strong></p>
    <p>Chapter 20: displacement or field → induced polarization and effective charge.</p>
    <p>Chapter 23: occupied Bloch subspace → Wannier centers.</p>
    <p>Chapter 24: transported charge / phase evolution → bulk polarization.</p>
  </aside>
  <p>
    This chapter supplies the missing bulk definition. It first explains why a unit-cell dipole is ambiguous, then replaces it with integrated bulk current. The phase structure of the occupied electronic wavefunctions turns that current integral into a Berry-phase expression, and the Wannier representation gives the same result as motion of localized charge centers. These ideas also connect polarization to localization and to the quantized transport that anticipates topology.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-24-2-fundamental-difficulty">
  <h2 id="section-24-2-fundamental-difficulty">24.2 Polarization: The Fundamental Difficulty</h2>
  <p>
    For a finite neutral object, the total dipole moment is well defined because the charge density vanishes outside a bounded region. A periodic solid has no such boundary-independent position operator. Choosing a different unit cell, or terminating a large sample on a different atomic plane, can change the apparent dipole per cell even though the interior bulk density is identical.
  </p>
  <p>
    Dividing a continuous electron density into atom-like or molecule-like pieces does not remove this ambiguity because the partition itself is not unique. A proper bulk formulation should depend only on periodic quantities in the interior and should not require an arbitrary surface termination or a privileged way of cutting the density into local dipoles.
  </p>
  <p>
    The local current provides that formulation. Let λ continuously parameterize an adiabatic change of the insulating Hamiltonian, for example an atomic displacement pattern. The macroscopic change in polarization is the charge transported through the bulk during that evolution:
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">P</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mi>d</mi><mi>λ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">J</mi><mi>mac</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>λ</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf P=\int_0^1 d\lambda\,\mathbf J_&#123;\mathrm&#123;mac&#125;&#125;(\lambda).</annotation></semantics></math>
  </div>
  <p>
    This statement matches experiment: switching a ferroelectric, straining a piezoelectric, or slowly moving sublattices produces a current whose time integral measures a change in polarization. It also establishes the central restriction of the bulk theory: the path must describe an insulating state continuously enough for adiabatic charge transport to be defined.
  </p>
  <p>
    The current formula solves the position-operator problem but still appears to depend on every intermediate point along λ. The next step is to show that quantum phase information reduces the result to a geometric difference between the endpoints, modulo an integer transported charge.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-24-3-berry-phase">
  <h2 id="section-24-3-berry-phase">24.3 Geometric Berry Phase Theory of Polarization</h2>
  <p>
    King-Smith and Vanderbilt’s construction expresses the electronic contribution to polarization through the phases of the occupied Bloch states. The relevant object is the Berry connection of the occupied subspace. In a smooth gauge it can be written schematically as
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">A</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>occ</mi></mrow></munder><mo>⟨</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>|</mo><msub><mo>∇</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></msub><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⟩</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf A(\mathbf k)=i\sum_&#123;n\in\mathrm&#123;occ&#125;&#125;\langle u_&#123;n\mathbf k&#125;|\nabla_\mathbf k|u_&#123;n\mathbf k&#125;\rangle.</annotation></semantics></math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The connection itself changes under a gauge transformation, so an arbitrary phase at one <em>k</em> point is not observable. A closed Brillouin-zone integral, or its multi-band determinant form, gives a Berry phase whose change is gauge invariant. For a three-dimensional insulator the electronic polarization can therefore be represented by the Brillouin-zone integral of the occupied connection,
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">P</mi><mi>el</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>e</mi><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>3</mn></msup></mfrac><msub><mo>∫</mo><mi>BZ</mi></msub><mi>d</mi><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">A</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mspace width="0.6em"/><mi>mod</mi><mspace width="0.3em"/><mfrac><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mi>cell</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf P_&#123;\mathrm&#123;el&#125;&#125;=-\frac&#123;e&#125;&#123;(2\pi)^3&#125;\int_&#123;\mathrm&#123;BZ&#125;&#125;d^3\mathbf k\,\mathbf A(\mathbf k)\quad\mathrm&#123;mod&#125;\;\frac&#123;e\mathbf R&#125;&#123;\Omega_&#123;\mathrm&#123;cell&#125;&#125;&#125;.</annotation></semantics></math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The final “modulo” is not a numerical nuisance. Changing the smooth gauge can shift the Berry phase by an integer multiple of 2π, corresponding physically to translating an integer charge through one lattice vector. The bulk crystal is unchanged after that operation, so polarization is intrinsically multivalued by a lattice quantum.
  </p>
  <p>
    Practical calculations use overlaps between occupied subspaces at neighboring <em>k</em> points rather than differentiating arbitrary eigenvector phases directly. Products of overlap matrices around a closed loop cancel local phase choices and retain the geometric phase. The broader gauge, curvature, and Wilson-loop framework is mapped in <a href={href('/theory/berry-phases-and-electronic-topology/')}>Berry Phases and Electronic Topology</a>.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-24-4-wannier-centers">
  <h2 id="section-24-4-wannier-centers">24.4 Relation to Centers of Wannier Functions</h2>
  <p>
    Chapter 23 showed that the first moment of a Wannier function is a Brillouin-zone integral of the same Berry connection. The polarization formula therefore has a direct real-space interpretation: the electronic contribution changes as the centers of the occupied Wannier functions move through the unit cell.
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>Δ</mi><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">P</mi><mi>el</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mi>cell</mi></msub></mfrac><munder><mo>∑</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>occ</mi></mrow></munder><mi>Δ</mi><msub><mover><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta\mathbf P_&#123;\mathrm&#123;el&#125;&#125;=-\frac&#123;e&#125;&#123;\Omega_&#123;\mathrm&#123;cell&#125;&#125;&#125;\sum_&#123;n\in\mathrm&#123;occ&#125;&#125;\Delta\bar&#123;\mathbf r&#125;_n.</annotation></semantics></math>
  </div>
  <p>
    Individual Wannier orbitals and individual centers depend on gauge, but the properly summed center displacement of the occupied subspace gives the same polarization change as the Berry phase. Moving a center by a lattice vector changes the chosen representative of the polarization by one quantum without changing the periodic bulk state.
  </p>
  <p>
    The total polarization also contains the ionic contribution from the nuclei or ionic cores. The useful mental picture is therefore a set of positive ionic charges plus electronic Wannier centers, with the important qualification that the separate electronic centers are representation dependent while the total change in polarization is the physical bulk quantity.
  </p>
  <p>
    Hybrid Wannier centers sharpen this connection further: localizing in one direction while retaining transverse crystal momentum turns the center into a function of the remaining momentum. Its evolution across the Brillouin zone becomes one of the most transparent ways to visualize the topological invariants introduced later.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-24-5-calculation">
  <h2 id="section-24-5-calculation">24.5 Calculation of Polarization in Crystals</h2>
  <p>
    Once polarization is defined as a Berry-phase quantity, ordinary structural response properties can be formulated without assigning static charges to atoms. A central example is the Born effective charge: the polarization response to displacement of atom κ in direction β,
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>α</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow><mo>*</mo></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mi>cell</mi></msub><mi>e</mi></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>α</mi></msub></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mi>β</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z^*_&#123;\kappa,\alpha\beta&#125;=\frac&#123;\Omega_&#123;\mathrm&#123;cell&#125;&#125;&#125;&#123;e&#125;\frac&#123;\partial P_\alpha&#125;&#123;\partial u_&#123;\kappa\beta&#125;&#125;.</annotation></semantics></math>
  </div>
  <p>
    Large or “anomalous” values can arise because an atomic displacement reorganizes covalent electronic charge and shifts Wannier centers substantially. This explains why an effective charge can differ strongly from an oxidation-state integer without any contradiction: the two quantities answer different questions.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same finite-change theory handles spontaneous polarization. For a ferroelectric with two symmetry-related polar structures, the experimentally relevant value follows from the branch-consistent change between the two states. For a pyroelectric or another polar crystal without an easily switchable partner, a theoretical reference structure can be connected to the physical structure along an insulating path and the Berry phase tracked continuously.
  </p>
  <aside class="callout" aria-label="Polarization calculation logic">
    <p><strong>Finite polarization change</strong></p>
    <p>choose two insulating endpoint structures → define a continuous insulating path → track Berry-phase branch → combine electronic and ionic changes → obtain ΔP.</p>
  </aside>
  <p>
    This is also the direct connection back to Chapter 20: dielectric response, effective charges, LO–TO splitting, piezoelectricity, and ferroelectric soft modes all depend on derivatives or finite changes of the same bulk polarization. The response framework is reviewed in <a href={href('/theory/linear-response-and-excited-states/')}>Linear Response and Excited States</a>.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-24-6-localization">
  <h2 id="section-24-6-localization">24.6 Localization: A Rigorous Measure</h2>
  <p>
    Kohn’s “insulating state” viewpoint associates insulation with localization of the many-electron ground state rather than with one particular orbital picture. The polarization framework makes that statement quantitative because fluctuations of the many-electron position—or equivalently fluctuations of polarization—remain bounded in an insulator and diverge as charge becomes macroscopically delocalized in a conductor.
  </p>
  <p>
    If <strong>X</strong> is the many-electron position along one direction, a localization length can be defined from the position variance per particle in the thermodynamic limit:
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ξ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>∞</mi></mrow></munder><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>N</mi></mfrac><mo>[</mo><mo>⟨</mo><msup><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>⟩</mo><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>⟩</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>]</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\xi^2=\lim_&#123;N\to\infty&#125;\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;N&#125;[\langle X^2\rangle-\langle X\rangle^2].</annotation></semantics></math>
  </div>
  <p>
    Through the fluctuation–dissipation relation, this localization measure is linked to the frequency-dependent optical conductivity. Low-frequency conducting weight causes the fluctuation to diverge, whereas an insulating optical gap allows a finite localization length. The definition therefore connects a ground-state geometric property to measurable response.
  </p>
  <p>
    In an independent-particle description the same invariant is related to the gauge-invariant part of the Wannier spread introduced in Chapter 23. This supplies physical meaning to that spread: it is not merely the width of a convenient localized orbital but a property of the occupied subspace that diagnoses how strongly electronic position can fluctuate outside it.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-24-7-thouless-pump">
  <h2 id="section-24-7-thouless-pump">24.7 The Thouless Quantized Particle Pump</h2>
  <p>
    A more dramatic consequence appears when the Hamiltonian is varied cyclically. Suppose λ returns to its initial value after one adiabatic cycle and the bulk gap stays open throughout. The final bulk Hamiltonian is then identical to the initial one, yet the continuously tracked Berry phase may wind by an integer multiple of 2π.
  </p>
  <p>
    That winding records an integer amount of charge transported through the crystal. For a one-dimensional pump the charge per cycle is
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.7em"/><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi mathvariant="double-struck">Z</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q=C e,\qquad C\in\mathbb&#123;Z&#125;.</annotation></semantics></math>
  </div>
  <p>
    The integer <em>C</em> is a Chern number defined over the two-dimensional parameter space formed by crystal momentum and the cyclic parameter. Quantization survives smooth changes of the Hamiltonian that do not close the gap, because such changes cannot continuously alter an integer topological invariant.
  </p>
  <p>
    The pump is therefore the first place in the main chapter sequence where the multivaluedness of polarization becomes a positive physical result rather than an ambiguity to manage. Returning to the same bulk branch modulo one quantum can still transfer a measurable integer charge between the ends of a finite system.
  </p>
</section>

<section aria-labelledby="section-24-8-polarization-lattice">
  <h2 id="section-24-8-polarization-lattice">24.8 Polarization Lattice</h2>
  <p>
    Because translating an electron by a lattice vector leaves the periodic bulk density unchanged, a crystal does not possess one unique vector value of polarization. Instead it possesses an equivalence class of values separated by polarization quanta:
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">P</mi><mo>≡</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">P</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>e</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mi>cell</mi></msub></mfrac><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.7em"/><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>lattice</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf P\equiv\mathbf P+\frac&#123;e\mathbf R&#125;&#123;\Omega_&#123;\mathrm&#123;cell&#125;&#125;&#125;,\qquad\mathbf R\in\mathrm&#123;lattice&#125;.</annotation></semantics></math>
  </div>
  <p>
    This “polarization lattice” is the correct bulk object. A finite experimental or computational change selects a branch by continuity from an initial state. If the change is small compared with the quantum, the appropriate branch is usually obvious; for large structural changes the Berry phase must be unwrapped along the path so that an integer branch jump is not mistaken for a giant physical polarization change.
  </p>
  <p>
    Symmetry constrains the allowed classes. In one dimension inversion sends <em>P</em> to −<em>P</em>, but the two values need only agree modulo the polarization quantum. The condition is therefore
  </p>
  <div class="math-display">
    <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>≡</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>P</mi><mspace width="0.5em"/><mo>(</mo><mi>mod</mi><mspace width="0.3em"/><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mo>)</mo><mspace width="0.8em"/><mo>⇒</mo><mspace width="0.5em"/><mi>P</mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width="0.3em"/><mi>or</mi><mspace width="0.3em"/><mfrac><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>q</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P\equiv-P\;(\mathrm&#123;mod&#125;\;P_q)\quad\Rightarrow\quad P\equiv0\;\mathrm&#123;or&#125;\;P_q/2.</annotation></semantics></math>
  </div>
  <p>
    Two inversion-symmetric insulating states can therefore belong to distinct geometric classes even though neither carries an arbitrary continuously variable polarization. Moving between such classes while preserving the relevant symmetry requires the insulating gap to close or the symmetry condition to be relaxed. This is the same kind of obstruction that Part VI will formalize using Berry curvature and topological invariants.
  </p>
</section>
